questions.detailed:
The goal is to let the browser paint within about 200 ms of an interaction. Work on three levers.

## 1. Do less work
- Ship less JavaScript (code splitting, tree shaking, dropping libraries) so there is less to evaluate and hydrate.
- Virtualise long lists and keep the DOM small.
- Remove or defer third-party code that runs on interactions.

## 2. Break up long tasks and yield
Tasks over 50 ms block input. Split loops and yield so the browser can paint:

```js
async function processItems(items) {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    handle(items[i]);
    // Yield every few items so input and rendering can run
    if (i % 50 === 0) {
      if ('scheduler' in globalThis && scheduler.yield) await scheduler.yield();
      else await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0));
    }
  }
}
```

## 3. Paint first, finish later
- Update the UI immediately (a pressed state, spinner, or optimistic result), then do expensive work after.
- In React, wrap non-urgent updates in `startTransition` or use `useDeferredValue` so typing and clicks stay responsive.
- Move heavy computation to a Web Worker when it is pure data work.

## React-specific checks
Colocate state, split contexts, avoid creating new object values on every render, and use `React.memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` **only when the Profiler shows they help**. They have their own cost and can hide the real issue.

## Defer non-critical work
Send analytics with batching, `navigator.sendBeacon` or after the paint, not inside the click path.

## Common mistakes
- Wrapping everything in memo "just in case".
- Using debounce as a cure when the real issue is a slow render.
- Optimising on a fast laptop and never re-testing with CPU throttling or RUM.
